</div></details>
<details class="panel sl-details"><summary>Costing &amp; how this connects to Accounting</summary><div class="panel-body sl-explain">
<div class="sl-grid"><div class="field"><label for="sl-set-rawcost">Farm cost of raw milk (Rs per litre)</label><input type="number" id="sl-set-rawcost" min="0" step="0.5" placeholder="0 = not set"></div><div class="field"><label>&nbsp;</label><button type="button" class="btn btn-green" id="sl-set-save">Save rate</button></div></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Sales</b> post once to Accounting as income (Milk sales — raw / pasteurized, Dairy product sales, Meat sales), with cash or bank received and any balance as a receivable.</li>
<li><b>Real spending</b> you enter here — pasteurization, bottles, cultures, slaughter fee, butcher — posts once as an expense, and is also added to the cost of the stock it made.</li>
<li><b>Stock cost</b> follows the milk and meat: raw milk at the rate above, pasteurized milk = raw milk used + processing, products = milk used + packaging + ingredients, carcass = animal cost + slaughter costs. Losses (pasteurization loss, bone and trim) stay in the cost of what remains.</li>
<li><b>Cost of goods sold and gross margin</b> use those stock costs. Feed, salaries and the animals themselves are already in Accounting as expenses, so stock costs are never booked as a second expense. Treat margins as estimates until the raw milk rate reflects your real cost.</li>
<li>Changing the rate affects milk received from now on; milk already in a tank keeps the cost it came in with.</li>
</ul></div></details>
</div>

